<article class="env-lemma" id="05DZ">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05DZ">Lemma <span data-tag="05DZ">10.63.13</span>.</a> Let $\varphi : R \to S$ be a ring map. Let $M$ be an $S$-module. If $S$ is Noetherian, then $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(\varphi )(\text{Ass}_ S(M)) = \text{Ass}_ R(M)$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We have already seen in Lemma <a href="/tag/05BW" data-tag="05BW">10.63.11</a> that $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(\varphi )(\text{Ass}_ S(M)) \subset \text{Ass}_ R(M)$. For the converse, choose a prime $\mathfrak p \in \text{Ass}_ R(M)$. Let $m \in M$ be an element such that the annihilator of $m$ in $R$ is $\mathfrak p$. Let $I = \{ g \in S \mid gm = 0\} $ be the annihilator of $m$ in $S$. Then $R/\mathfrak p \subset S/I$ is injective. Combining Lemmas <a href="/tag/00FK" data-tag="00FK">10.30.5</a> and <a href="/tag/0CAN" data-tag="0CAN">10.30.7</a> we see that there is a prime $\mathfrak q \subset S$ minimal over $I$ mapping to $\mathfrak p$. By Proposition <a href="/tag/02CE" data-tag="02CE">10.63.6</a> we see that $\mathfrak q$ is an associated prime of $S/I$, hence $\mathfrak q$ is an associated prime of $M$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/02M3" data-tag="02M3">10.63.3</a> and we win. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>